+    <h2>1. Privacy by Design &amp; Data Minimization</h2>
+    <p>
+        The DevTools Times is built around <strong>Privacy by Design</strong>, <strong>Data Minimization</strong>, and <strong>User Transparency</strong>. By default, you can browse articles, reports, and multimedia without being tracked by third-party analytics or advertising scripts, and without unnecessary session cookies being created.
+    </p>
+
+    <h2>2. Data We Collect &amp; Purpose of Processing</h2>
+    <p><strong>Account Sign-Up and Sign-In</strong></p>
+    <ul>
+        <li><strong>Minimal Data Collection:</strong> When you create an account or sign in, we only collect a <strong>username</strong> and <strong>password</strong>. We do not require your real name, email address, phone number, or demographic data.</li>
+        <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> Your credentials are used strictly to authenticate your account. They are never sold, rented, or shared with advertisers.</li>
+        <li><strong>Credential Protection:</strong> Sign-in and sign-up requests are sent with <code>credentials: 'omit'</code> and <code>referrerPolicy: 'no-referrer'</code>, form fields are cleared from memory immediately after submission, authentication tokens are not persisted in <code>localStorage</code>, and all authentication interactions are excluded from analytics via <code>data-skip-tracking="true"</code>.</li>
+    </ul>
+
+    <h2>3. Sessions, Cookies &amp; Local Storage</h2>
+    <p><strong>Lazy Reading List Sessions (Strictly Necessary)</strong></p>
+    <p>
+        We do not initialize a server session or set session cookies when you simply visit or read The DevTools Times. A functional session (<code>readingList</code>) is created <strong>only</strong> when you actively bookmark an article to save it to your reading list.
+    </p>
+    <p><strong>Privacy Consent Preferences</strong></p>
+    <p>
+        Your privacy choices are stored locally in your browser under the <code>devtools-times-privacy-consent</code> key in <code>localStorage</code> so we can respect your opt-in or opt-out decision across page loads.
